    passage:
      "Behavioral psychologists often describe habits as a loop with three stages: cue, routine, and reward. The cue triggers the behavior. The routine is the behavior itself. The reward tells the brain whether the loop is worth remembering. Over time, this loop becomes automatic. The brain stops analyzing and starts following.\n\nThis mechanism is useful. It allows you to drive a car without thinking about every gear change. It allows you to type a message without looking at each key. Efficiency, however, has a shadow. The same loop that makes good habits durable also makes bad habits persistent.\n\nSmoking, scrolling, procrastination, and negative self-talk often follow the same pattern. They begin with a cue: stress, boredom, or anxiety. They continue with a routine: reaching for a phone, opening a browser, or saying 'I will do it later.' They end with a reward: distraction, comfort, or temporary relief.\n\nChanging a habit is rarely about willpower. It is about redesigning the loop. Keep the cue. Keep the reward. Change the routine. If stress triggers the need for relief, replace scrolling with five deep breaths or a short walk. The brain can learn a new routine for the same cue and reward.\n\nThis is not fast. It is not easy. But it is evidence-based. And for many people, it is the first realistic explanation of why change feels so difficult — and how it can still happen.",
    translation:
      "行为心理学家常常把习惯描述为一个包含三个阶段的循环：提示、惯例和奖励。提示触发行为。惯例是行为本身。奖励告诉大脑这个循环是否值得记住。随着时间推移，这个循环变成自动的。大脑停止分析，开始遵循。\n\n这个机制是有用的。它允许你开车时不必思考每一次换挡。它允许你打字时不必看每个键。然而，效率有一个阴影。让好习惯持久的同一个循环也让坏习惯持续存在。\n\n吸烟、刷手机、拖延和消极的自我对话常常遵循相同的模式。它们从提示开始：压力、无聊或焦虑。它们以惯例继续：拿起手机、打开浏览器或者说'我稍后再做'。它们以奖励结束：分心、安慰或暂时的解脱。\n\n改变习惯很少关于意志力。它关于重新设计循环。保留提示。保留奖励。改变惯例。如果压力触发对解脱的需求，用五次深呼吸或短途散步代替刷手机。大脑可以学会为同一个提示和奖励设计一套新惯例。\n\n这并不快。这不容易。但它是基于证据的。对许多人来说，这是第一个现实的解释：为什么改变感觉如此困难——以及它如何仍然可以发生。",
